<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristagoras of Miletus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 497 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristagoras was tyrant of Miletus, ruling in place of his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaeus">Histiaeus</a> under Persian authority. After a failed expedition against Naxos he led the Ionian cities into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 499 BCE and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. He left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, where he was killed, and Persia sacked Miletus in 494 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus.jpg" alt="Aristagoras of Miletus" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IONIA,_Miletos._5th_century_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
